Q: How to set start page in Web.config file in asp.net c# A: <p>I think this will help</p>
<pre><code>&lt;system.webServer&gt; 
    &lt;directoryBrowse enabled=&quot;false&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;defaultDocument&gt;
      &lt;files&gt;
        &lt;clear /&gt;
        &lt;add value=&quot;index.aspx&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;add value=&quot;Default.htm&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;add value=&quot;Default.asp&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;add value=&quot;index.htm&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;add value=&quot;index.html&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;add value=&quot;iisstart.htm&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;add value=&quot;default.aspx&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;add value=&quot;index.php&quot; /&gt;
      &lt;/files&gt;
    &lt;/defaultDocument&gt;
&lt;/system.webServer&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Hide text, leave icon elements A: <p>Some additional notes on the previous approaches:</p>
<h3>Hide via <code>font-size</code></h3>
<p>As suggested by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/34000189/15015675">Rokin</a> you can easily override a child elements font-size in a parent element with <code>font-size:0</code>.</p>
<p>However, you may run into troubles because most icon libraries deploy CSS rules applying font-size inheritance:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>html {
  font-size: 32px;
}

/* just to emulate window resize */
.action-bar {
  resize: both;
  overflow: auto;
  border: 4px dotted #ccc;
  padding:0.2em;
  container-type: inline-size;
}


.btn {
  font-size: 1em;
  display: inline-block;
}



@container (max-inline-size: 480px) {

  /* hide button text */
  .btn {
    font-size: 0;
  }

  /* override font-size */
  .fa {
    font-size: initial;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/font-awesome@4.7.0/css/font-awesome.css"&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resize me&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="action-bar"&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-home"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 1
    &lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-arrow-right"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 2
    &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>In the above snippet the icons also inherit <code>font-size:0</code> due to default fontAwesome style rules (abbreviated):</p>
<pre><code>.fa {
    display: inline-block;
    line-height: 1;
    font-family: FontAwesome;
    font-size: inherit;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Workaround</strong> just add another rule with higher <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Cascade/Specificity" rel="nofollow noreferrer">specificity</a> like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>/* 2 selectors - higher specificity */
.btn .fa {
  font-size: 1rem;
}
</code></pre>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>html {
  font-size: 32px;
}

/* just to emulate window resize */
.action-bar {
  resize: both;
  overflow: auto;
  border: 4px dotted #ccc;
  padding:0.2em;
  container-type: inline-size;
}


.btn {
  font-size: 1em;
  display: inline-block;
}


/* override font-size */
.btn .fa {
  font-size: 1rem;
}

@container (max-inline-size: 480px) {

  /* hide button text */
  .btn {
    font-size: 0;
  }

}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/font-awesome@4.7.0/css/font-awesome.css"&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resize me&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="action-bar"&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-home"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 1
    &lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-arrow-right"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 2
    &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>However, this introduces another problem: we can't use relative font-sizes inherited from the parent element. Instead we need to use more explicit size values such as <code>rem</code> (relative to HTML root) or <code>px</code>.</p>
<h3>Hide via »cropping«</h3>
<p>As described by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/34000082/15015675">Mr Lister</a> we may simply crop the button element itself to a specific rectangle.</p>
<p>Since most icon libraries are designed using a square reference frame, we can simply set dimension so e.g <code>width:1em; height:1em</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.btn {
  font-size: 1em;
  display: inline-block;
  overflow: hidden;
}


@media (max-width: 480px) {
  .btn {
    width: 1em;
    height: 1em;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>html {
  font-size: 32px;
}

/* just to emulate window resize */
.action-bar {
  resize: both;
  overflow: auto;
  border: 4px dotted #ccc;
  padding: 0.2em;
  container-type: inline-size;
}


.btn {
  font-size: 1em;
  display: inline-block;
  overflow: hidden;
}



@container (max-inline-size: 480px) {

  /* crop button text */
  .btn {
    width: 1em;
    height: 1em;
  }

}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/font-awesome@4.7.0/css/font-awesome.css"&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resize me&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="action-bar"&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-home"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 1
    &lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;a href="" class="btn btn-primary"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa fa-arrow-right"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; 
        Button 2
    &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: SWC with JavaScript: How to handle CSS imports and how to absolute imports? A: <p>I was able to solve all issues without writing any plugins. I pushed the solution to <a href="https://github.com/janhesters/riteway-swc-example" rel="nofollow noreferrer">my example repo</a> from the question.</p>
<p>Firstly, use the official <a href="https://github.com/swc-project/register" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SWC project's register</a>. This means, you have to compile your tests like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;test&quot;: &quot;riteway -r @swc/register 'src/**/*.test.js' | tap-nirvana&quot;,
</code></pre>
<p>You can install it by running:</p>
<pre><code>yarn add --dev @swc/core @swc/register
</code></pre>
<p>We need this version of register, because it takes into account an <code>.swcrc</code> file, which the other one did not.</p>
<p>Secondly, you need both <code>&quot;path&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;baseUrl&quot;</code> to fix the absolute imports because for some reason SWC doesn't infer all paths with only a <code>&quot;baseUrl&quot;</code> provided. And you need to adapt your <code>.swcrc</code> to handle React.</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;jsc&quot;: {
    &quot;baseUrl&quot;: &quot;./src&quot;,
    &quot;paths&quot;: {
      &quot;*.css&quot;: [&quot;utils/identity-object-proxy.js&quot;],
      &quot;utils/*&quot;: [&quot;utils/*&quot;]
    },
    &quot;parser&quot;: {
      &quot;jsx&quot;: true,
      &quot;syntax&quot;: &quot;ecmascript&quot;
    },
    &quot;transform&quot;: {
      &quot;react&quot;: {
        &quot;runtime&quot;: &quot;automatic&quot;
      }
    }
  },
  &quot;module&quot;: {
    &quot;type&quot;: &quot;commonjs&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Thirdly, to solve <code>.css</code> you need to remap all imports to <code>.css</code> files to an identity object proxy, which you can see in the <code>.swcrc</code> example above. An identity object proxy is an object that, when you reference any property, returns the stringified key that you're trying to reference. You can create one yourself like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const identityObjectProxy = new Proxy(
  {},
  {
    get: function getter(target, key) {
      if (key === '__esModule') {
        return false;
      }
      return key;
    },
  },
);

export default identityObjectProxy;
</code></pre>
<p>For the <code>.css</code> remap to go into effect you need to make all your imports to <code>.css</code> files absolute imports. (<code>import styles from './styles.module.css</code> won't work!)</p>
